    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "What is the maximum value of $-x^2+3x-1$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac54$, attained when $x=\\dfrac32$.",
      "worked": "Let $y=-x^2+3x-1$. Since the coefficient of $x^2$ is negative, the vertex gives the maximum.\n$$\\begin{align*}x_{\\mathrm{vertex}}&=-\\frac{3}{2(-1)}\\\\x_{\\mathrm{vertex}}&=\\frac32\\\\y_{\\mathrm{vertex}}&=-\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2+3\\left(\\frac32\\right)-1\\\\y_{\\mathrm{vertex}}&=-\\frac94+\\frac92-1\\\\y_{\\mathrm{vertex}}&=\\frac54\\end{align*}$$\nThe maximum value is $\\dfrac54$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 35
  },
